1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:28,000
It's no secret that our society has a digital device problem. Ninety-two percent of Americans own a smartphone. Research has found that we are more connected than ever before and yet feel more isolated, more lonely, more depressed than ever before. How is that possible?

2
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Social scientists call this a collective action problem. We all agree that these devices are having a detrimental impact on our mental health, and yet it feels virtually impossible to withdraw from these devices because they are so interconnected in our lives. So what can we do?

3
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Well, good morning. Wonderful to see all of you this morning. If we haven't had the privilege of meeting yet,

4
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:08,000
my name is Ryan, and I am privileged to be one of the pastors here. And we are in week three of a four-week series on digital detox and the influence of modern technology on our spiritual lives.

5
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:20,000
And attendance is looking light. So I don't know if that means a lot of people are traveling for the summertime or you guys are sick of talking about your phones,

6
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
but we're in the homestretch. We're getting close. And as a part of this series, we're inviting you all to participate in a 28-day digital detox.

7
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,000
You guys have heard, most of you have heard this already. There's an insert in your bulletin that can help you unpack some of the ways some of us are participating in this.

8
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And again, we've said this from the beginning, we are not anti-tech, but we do acknowledge over the past two weeks,

9
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
we've talked about the fact that technology can be a very effective tool in conforming us into the patterns of this world.

10
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
And it also amplifies the voice. Last week we talked about it amplifying the voice of thieves and robbers who seek to steal, kill, and destroy our lives,

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
over and above the voice of the Good Shepherd who offers us life and life more abundantly.

12
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
So yeah, that's where we've been, and we are in the homestretch looking forward to that.

13
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
I do want to do one more shameless plug for Team Night. We have some big news that we're going to be announcing at Team Night,

14
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
and we like to give big news to our volunteers and our members first. And so I just want you to know that's coming.

15
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
I know not all of you can make it, but if you can, please try. And I'd love for that to happen.

16
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Okay, let's pray. Holy Spirit, we just submit this to you.

17
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Submit this time to you moving forward. Pray that you would just be at work through everything that happens under this roof this morning,

18
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
that you would have your way, that you would prepare our hearts, that you would speak through your word

19
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
and through other believers to open our eyes to see where you're at work.

20
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Ask us questions that we don't want to be asked. Challenge us where we don't want to be challenged.

21
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:21,000
And ultimately, God, I pray that this time together would be a step forward in our surrendered obedience to you.

22
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:29,000
For those that are in here that don't yet know you, that are just kind of kicking the tires of Christianity and are curious about your word,

23
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:38,000
I pray that this would be a really clear proclamation of who they are, who they were made to be, and what you're inviting them into moving forward.

24
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
I pray all of this in Jesus' name. Amen.

25
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
We're going to be in Genesis 1 through 3. So if you want to turn there, it shouldn't be hard to find. If you're new to the Bible, it's at the very beginning.

26
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So we're making it easy for you this morning. As you're turning there, I would love to ask you all a question.

27
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Have any of you ever ignored your GPS because you were convinced that you knew a better way?

28
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
All right. Now, do me a favor. Leave your hand up if that had ever backfired on you.

29
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Okay. Some of you are smarter than the GPS than others. I remember a while back, our in-laws were coming to visit us.

30
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:25,000
We knew their kind of estimated time of arrival. And so when time was getting close for them to show up, we have some location sharing.

31
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And so my wife checked her sister's location, and they were still about an hour and a half away, which was not their ETA.

32
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
And so Marissa texted her sister, and her sister is very funny.

33
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
And so she was in the backseat with mom and dad in the front seat, and she gave a hilarious commentary.

34
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
I don't remember all the specifics, but it was something like the GPS was trying to take them a different way than they normally go.

35
00:04:49,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And so they just decided to go the way they normally go. They ran into, I think it was traffic, and then they got so stuck in traffic that they tried to, again, ignore the rerouting of the GPS

36
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:14,000
and get back to the original route. And when they got back to the original route, there was an accident, and then at that point, it was just all, you know, the wheels were off, quite literally.

37
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And so it was just a wild time. And I realized the issue for them wasn't the destination. The destination had not changed, but it was the guide that they were putting their trust in.

38
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And then having a GPS that had moment-by-moment connection to satellite and updates with traffic and construction and all the things to like an old-school Atlas navigation.

39
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Now, I realized that if you were here for last week's message, an opening illustration about trusting technology, slightly ironic on my part. I get that.

40
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:59,000
But I think it's a helpful picture for our passage this morning, because last week we talked about this passage in John 10,

41
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:07,000
where there are thieves and robbers who come to us with a voice that is opposite of the voice of the Good Shepherd.

42
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And these voices lead to our lives being stolen and ultimately led astray from where God wants us to.

43
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:25,000
And we said that at the end of the day that there are lots of thieves and robbers, but all of those voices can be traced back to the original voice of the thief in the garden.

44
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:38,000
And today what we're going to do is we're going to look at that origin story of the original voice of the thief so that we can better understand how our screens and our devices are still amplifying that voice in our lives regularly.

45
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
And I think what we're going to see is that the destination for us as human beings, from the very beginning, the destination has not changed.

46
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
We are still going in the same direction. We are still trying to achieve the thing that God made us to achieve.

47
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:56,000
But the guide in which we put our trust in to accomplish those things has changed.

48
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
So let's read in Genesis chapter 1, starting in verse 26.

49
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
We're going to hop around to a couple of different places.

50
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Genesis 1, starting in verse 26, it says, "Then God said, 'Let us make man in our image, after our likeness, and let them have dominion over the fish of the sea,

51
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:22,000
and over the birds of the heavens, and over the livestock, and over all the earth, and over every creeping thing that creeps on the earth.

52
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:29,000
So God created man in his own image. In the image of God, he created them male and female, he created them.

53
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:35,000
And God blessed them, and God said to them, 'Be fruitful and multiply, fill the earth and subdue it.

54
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Have dominion over the fish of the sea, and over the birds of the heavens, and over every living thing that moves on earth.'"

55
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:53,000
We skip to chapter 2, verse 15, that says, "The Lord God took the man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work it and keep it."

56
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:01,000
And so, from the very beginning, we get this idea of the destination that God created humanity for.

57
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,000
At the very beginning, God lays out his intended destination for us as humans, that we are created in his image.

58
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:14,000
We are given dominion over his creation. This is, from the beginning, all of us, this is our identity and our calling.

59
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Our identity is image bearers of the Creator God, and our calling is to have dominion over creation.

60
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:33,000
God plants a garden of abundance for them, and he gives them the responsibility and the authority to expand the goodness of Eden across the rest of the world.

61
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:43,000
The rest of the world was full of potential, but not yet like Eden, and so God commissions and sets apart these image bearers who would image him to the rest of creation.

62
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:57,000
They would expand Eden moving forward. The term here that we see in Genesis 2:15, to work it and keep it, is a phrase that is later used of the priests in the tabernacle.

63
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And so the original calling for humans was to worship God in the way in which we worked throughout creation.

64
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:14,000
I think that's a really beautiful reminder for those of us that we often shrink down the definition of what worship is.

65
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:23,000
It becomes this very spiritual term that only happens in certain contexts, but from the beginning, God was inviting us to worship him in our vocation, in our calling.

66
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:34,000
And so whatever it is that you might do on a regular basis, there are opportunities for you to worship God in what looks like ordinary, everyday tasks.

67
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So our identity, our calling from the very beginning is to image God in the way that we expand Eden throughout creation.

68
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:52,000
And as we read ahead in the story, we'll see, we've talked about this previously, it becomes clear that even after the fall, even after sin enters the world,

69
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:02,000
the destination doesn't change. Humans are still worshiping. We are still seeking to create Eden-like abundance in the world around us.

70
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:09,000
We are still trying to do the thing that we were made for. The destination has not changed.

71
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:19,000
What has changed is the guide in which we choose to put our trust in to get to that destination, which leads us to the weird death tree.

72
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Okay? Everybody loves the death tree. Genesis 2:15-17.

73
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Again, the Lord God took man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work it and keep it.

74
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:37,000
And the Lord God commanded the man, saying, "You may surely eat of every tree of the garden,

75
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:46,000
but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat, for in the day you eat of it you will surely die."

76
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:51,000
The Hebrew literally says, "Die, die."

77
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:56,000
When you eat of it, you will die, die. Not a good tree, apparently, right?

78
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:02,000
So I don't know about you, and I don't know that I was ever taught this or if I just assumed this,

79
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:09,000
but for most of my life when reading this text, I came to the assumption or the conclusion or the thought process

80
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
that the knowledge of good and evil is a bad thing.

81
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Does anybody else feel like they grew up with that idea, the knowledge of good and evil, bad thing?

82
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Okay. I mean, look at the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, right?

83
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
If you eat of it, you will die, die. That seems like a bad thing,

84
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
but the more that I've thought about this and looked at it and studied it and been influenced by other people,

85
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:36,000
I don't think that that's what we are trying to get across in Genesis chapter 2.

86
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:41,000
This phrase, knowledge of good and evil, is used three other times in the Hebrew Bible,

87
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:49,000
and all three times it is a positive thing where people who need wisdom are gaining in wisdom, learning wisdom.

88
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:59,000
It becomes an idea of some adolescent or child or innocent person who doesn't have wisdom and needs wisdom and gets wisdom.

89
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:05,000
The clearest example in the Scriptures is King Solomon. You guys remember King Solomon, David's son?

90
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:10,000
What happens to Solomon is he falls asleep and he has a dream and God comes to Solomon and God says,

91
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,000
that you can ask for anything that you want and I will give it to you.

92
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:25,000
We remember the story? Some of us. Okay. So literally, you can read it in first Kings chapter three, Solomon asks for the knowledge of good and evil.

93
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:33,000
And God is very pleased. And so I think what we need to get in our minds here is it's not the knowledge of good and evil that is a bad thing.

94
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:39,000
The knowledge of good and evil is wisdom. It's wisdom that Adam and Eve would need to fulfill their calling.

95
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Wisdom that they had already begun to receive from their creator. Throughout Genesis, we see God declaring things as good.

96
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:54,000
And then we see God declaring something as not good. He is defining for them what the definition of good and evil is, and he would continue to do so.

97
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:02,000
We see him walking to them in the garden. That there is this relationship of vulnerability and trust, and that he is giving them what they need.

98
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:08,000
The wisdom that they need as they need it to accomplish the thing that he created them for.

99
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:20,000
This is a tree of wisdom, but the problem is, who are we trusting to get our wisdom from?

100
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Which leads us to the origin story of the original thief in Genesis chapter 3.

101
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Let's read it, and then we'll talk about how weird it is. Okay.

102
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,000
"Now, the serpent was more crafty than any other beast of the field that the Lord God had made.

103
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:43,000
He said to the woman, "Did God actually say, 'You shall not eat of any tree in the garden?'"

104
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:55,000
And the woman said to the serpent, "We may eat of the fruit of the trees in the garden, but God said, 'You shall not eat of the fruit of the tree that is in the middle of the garden, neither shall you touch it, lest you die-die.'"

105
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Sorry, I really like that. That's so fun.

106
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:06,000
"But the serpent said to the woman, 'You will not die-die, for God knows that when you eat, your eyes will be opened, and you will be like God,

107
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
knowing good and evil.'" So when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, that it was a delight to the eyes,

108
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,000
and that the tree was to be desired to make one wise, she took its fruit and ate.

109
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:25,000
She also gave some to her husband, who was with her, and he ate. Then the eyes of both of them were opened.

110
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:34,000
They knew that they were naked, and so they sewed fig leaves together and made themselves loincloths."

111
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:43,000
All right, can we just be straight up? Talking snake is weird.

112
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:49,000
And the fact that those of us who grew up in the church pretend that it's not weird is also weird.

113
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
We're just like, "Yeah, of course, there's a talking snake at the beginning of creation.

114
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,000
It's supposed to happen. It's normal. It's not normal. It's weird."

115
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:04,000
And we could literally, we could do a whole sermon or sermon series on the snake and all of the

116
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
cultural ideas around it and what it means and why it means. We don't have time to get into that.

117
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:18,000
I think for now what I want to say is the snake seems to be some type of spiritual being, divine being.

118
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:26,000
It doesn't, the Hebrew allows for it to not just be wiser than any other beast in the field.

119
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Like, it is a beast that's wiser than the other beast. It just allows it to be just wiser than the other creatures.

120
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:38,000
And we know from other Scriptures that there are divine beings that exist that are interacting with God.

121
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:43,000
There's a spiritual realm and a physical realm. We've talked about all of these things in the past.

122
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,000
I don't have time to get into them right now, but for now I will say that the snake seems to be a divine being,

123
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:55,000
and it also seems to have more wisdom than the other creatures.

124
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:02,000
And even the way that our translators kind of lead us down a path, it says crafty.

125
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:08,000
It sounds morally negative. I'm told that the Hebrew is very morally neutral.

126
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:12,000
It is wisdom that can be used for positive or for negative.

127
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:18,000
And so we have a wise spiritual being coming to Eve and beginning to question God's wisdom.

128
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:25,000
So he's using his wisdom in a negative way, and he offers her a shortcut for their intended destination.

129
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:31,000
He's coming to them. He says, "I know what you were made for. I know what God has created you to be."

130
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:37,000
The way in which he's giving you the wisdom you need to rule the creation that he's called you to is setting you back.

131
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:44,000
There's a shortcut that you can take. There's a faster route to get to the destination that God has laid out for you.

132
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:52,000
He essentially says you can have the thing you'll need to do the thing God made you to do, and you can have it right now.

133
00:16:52,000 --> 00:17:00,000
You don't have to wait for God to teach you wisdom. You can seize it for yourself and immediately become like God.

134
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:06,000
You can have God's promises apart from God's presence.

135
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:12,000
And what comes next in my mind is one of the most tragic grammar transitions in all of Scripture.

136
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Because again, up to this point in the Genesis account, God has been the one who is seeing things and defining if they are good or not.

137
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:29,000
All throughout Genesis 1, God sees it's good. God sees it's good. God sees it's very good.

138
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Then he gets to Adam being alone, and God sees that it's not good. God is the one who is seeing and defining what is good and what is bad.

139
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:46,000
And what we see in this transition when the serpent comes to Eve and just offers her this idea that she might be able to define wisdom for herself.

140
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:55,000
Verse 6 says, "The woman saw the tree was good for food."

141
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:13,000
So all the way out through the text, God is seeing and defining what is good, and now after this hint of this temptation, this discussion, this decision for Adam and Eve to be made, now Eve sees something that God had said wasn't good, and she begins to declare it good in her own eyes.

142
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:20,000
She begins the process right here and now of trusting her own definition of good and evil apart from God.

143
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:32,000
Her and Adam both participate in this choice together and the result is immediate death-death, okay? They did indeed die-die.

144
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:44,000
This is the result. Death doesn't come when they get kicked out of the garden, but it comes the moment that they begin to choose to trust their own wisdom apart from God.

145
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:52,000
It's important that we know that God does not kill Adam and Eve. The fruit from the tree does not kill Adam and Eve.

146
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:58,000
It is their decision to take the ability to define good and evil for themselves that kills them.

147
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:11,000
And as we unpack why that might be, I think it's really important that up to this point, the world is being governed by God's wisdom, and God's wisdom accounts for everyone and everything in creation.

148
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:24,000
It cultivates mutually beneficial and life-giving morality that benefits everyone equally. God's wisdom is bigger and better than limited human wisdom.

149
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:33,000
When we take wisdom for ourselves, when we define it for ourselves, it limits the scope and the perspective of that definition of wisdom.

150
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:40,000
And then what happens immediately, and we see this in the garden, is what I define as good is often at odds with what my neighbor defines as good.

151
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:45,000
And then we become at odds with one another.

152
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:51,000
And now we're all defining wisdom for ourselves. We're all defining good and evil for ourselves.

153
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
And the immediate result is death. Adam and Eve stopped.

154
00:19:54,000 --> 00:20:00,000
When Adam and Eve stopped viewing vulnerability and mutual partnership as a virtue, death occurred.

155
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
They moved from co-submitted partners that are under God's authority in a world full of abundance.

156
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:16,000
This is their existence, co-submitted partners, both under the defining wisdom of God working together to bring about Eden-like circumstances in the world around them.

157
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:24,000
They moved from that to autonomous and a fractured partnership in a world where I heard one pastor define it as protective scarcity.

158
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:31,000
Immediately, the move becomes God is in charge. He's telling us what to do. We're waiting on him. Everything around us looks great.

159
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:40,000
There's abundance everywhere, and in that moment, everything shrinks down, and all they can think about is how they're going to get what they need for their calling and their identity,

160
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:52,000
and we are immediately moved from abundance to protective scarcity. It feels like there isn't enough, and it feels like other people are making decisions about what is good and bad for them,

161
00:20:52,000 --> 00:21:01,000
and their decisions about what is good and evil is limiting my potential to get the thing that I need to define what I have as good and evil.

162
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:17,000
That same pastor who defined protective scarcity, he talked about the image of God as both a noun and a verb, and so what doesn't happen at the fall when we die-die is we don't stop being noun images of God.

163
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:28,000
God created all humanity, all humankind in His image. We image God. We are noun images of God. The calling and vocation that He put on our lives to worship

164
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and to cultivate Eden-like circumstances, none of that goes away. The destination does not go away, but we now can choose whether we will verb image God or not.

165
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:55,000
And when we stop being verb images of God, the result is regular death. My overarching point for this morning is this. The fall wasn't humanity choosing evil over good. It was humanity

166
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:14,000
choosing good apart from God. This is what we see, that the destination doesn't change. Humans are still worshiping. They're still trying to cultivate Eden-like circumstances in the world around them, but they're choosing to do something that is good that God made them for apart from God, and that always leads to death.

167
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:23,000
If you will allow me, I will read a long quote because I think it summarizes this far better than I could ever say it. Does that sound okay?

